1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
A story as old as civilization.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
A simple alchemy of earth, water and fire.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:13,000
A timeless handicraft that has progressed to a mass production miracle.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
A durable building block.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And literally, the brick and mortar of great cities.

6
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Bricks on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Towering 450 feet into the sky is Western Kentucky Energy's newest chimney.

8
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,140
From the outside, it may look like just another concrete structure.

9
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:57,180
However, inside the concrete shell is a crucial 30-foot diameter brick lining.

10
00:00:57,181 --> 00:01:03,060
Western Kentucky Energy's chimney liner will require nearly 350,000 bricks,

11
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,860
210,000 pounds of mortar,

12
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,640
and the sweat of the bricklayers that put it all together.

13
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,240
Each brick weighs approximately 11 pounds,

14
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,940
and each bricklayer lays about 1,000 bricks a day,

15
00:01:26,941 --> 00:01:31,100
so during the course of a day he's lifting approximately 11,000 pounds.

16
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:35,080
Get you right in here and get you right in here.

17
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:39,120
It just takes a lot of time and practice.

18
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:43,400
I mean, if you do it every day, every day you get better and better at it.

19
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:46,560
It's kind of exciting being up this high, up in the air.

20
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:53,200
The challenge of the job itself, you can say that you've done something at the end of the job.

21
00:01:54,420 --> 00:01:55,420
Each and every day.

22
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:02,280
The brick will protect the concrete from the caustic gases exhausted from the daily operation of the power plant.

23
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:08,060
Older brick chimneys were constructed with outer brick walls and inner brick liners.

24
00:02:08,061 --> 00:02:13,060
The outer wall was a common brick or radial brick without acid-resistant qualities.

25
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:21,740
Concrete has replaced brick on the exterior, but the internal wall still needs to be an acid-resistant material.

26
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:25,040
And here, brick is still king.

27
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:33,040
The flue gas contains sulfur oxides from the combustion of coal in the boilers.

28
00:02:33,041 --> 00:02:37,960
It's cleaned through the use of the flue gas to sulfurization system, but what results

29
00:02:37,961 --> 00:02:44,760
is a highly saturated flue gas with a sulfuric acid condensate in it, which is highly corrosive

30
00:02:44,761 --> 00:02:48,000
to any materials that it comes in contact with.

31
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:52,720
Concrete alone would not be resistant at all to the acid in the flue gas and would start

32
00:02:52,721 --> 00:02:54,360
to deteriorate almost immediately.

33
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,200
But the brick used is not just any ordinary brick.

34
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:00,120
It's acid-proof brick.

35
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:05,440
Acid-proof brick is essentially the same brick that we find in our homes.

36
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:10,840
However, it contains a shale clay, a geologically compressed material,

37
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:13,440
which makes it resistant to chemical assaults.

38
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:16,120
But even acid brick is just a brick.

39
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,080
To become a brick wall, you need mortar.

40
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:21,880
And in this case, an acid-proof mortar.

41
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:25,700
The chimney brick is laid in a potassium silicate mortar,

42
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:29,480
which is highly resistant to the sulfuric acid in the flue gas.

43
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:33,420
Ordinary Portland cement mortar would last only a few weeks in this environment.

44
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,220
This mortar is a lot different than regular mortar.

45
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:40,200
You put on a thin layer, whereas when you're laying different brick,

46
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,260
you have to lay thicker mortar joints.

47
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:48,620
With 300 feet of wall left to go, the brick layers are just starting to feel the burn.

48
00:03:48,621 --> 00:03:52,860
I probably will have laid about 70 or 80,000 brick by the end of this project.

49
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,060
It'll be a memory that, you know, will go on forever.

50
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:58,500
Every time I drive by, I say, yeah, I was part of that project.

51
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:04,580
The history of brick is the story of civilization.

52
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:10,180
And it has been said that architecture itself began when two bricks were put together well.

53
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,520
Although many great civilizations have risen and fallen,

54
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,680
The narrative of their ingenuity can be found in the brick and mortar structures

55
00:04:18,681 --> 00:04:21,520
that defiantly stand against time and the elements.

56
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,840
Brick is one of the oldest building materials manufactured by man.

57
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:30,140
You have bricks they found that are as old as 10,000 years old.

58
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:34,980
So it's been something that people understood as a technology from the very beginning.

59
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:39,700
The earliest bricks we find in the late Stone Age period, Neolithic period,

60
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:43,300
are simply molded with the hands into a rough loaf shape

61
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:48,860
and then stacked up to make a house and then covered up with mud daubed on the outside.

62
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:53,120
The first advancement in brickmaking, the wooden mold,

63
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:58,140
was developed sometime after the Stone Age and was utilized extensively by the ancient Egyptians.

64
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:06,400
With this primitive tool, a single brickmaker gained the ability to mold hundreds of identical rectangular bricks in one day.

65
00:05:07,040 --> 00:05:09,780
If you look at the older bricks, you know, some of the ancient Egyptians

66
00:05:09,781 --> 00:05:12,840
or some of the things that happened when brickmaking first occurred,

67
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,560
It came about as something that would fit a person's hand.

68
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:19,260
And it was made that way, obviously, for efficiency.

69
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:24,280
The brick of the ancient Egyptians has changed very little through the years.

70
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:32,040
And the mud brick they used is still in use today and is commonly referred to as adobe.

71
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,700
To make adobe brick, mud is mixed with straw to add strength and to control shrinking and cracking.

72
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:42,980
The mixture is put into a mold and shaped.

73
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,820
And then the soft brick is set out in the sun to dry.

74
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:53,400
But one drawback to this simple building block is that it can be destroyed by water.

75
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:58,460
So perhaps the greatest innovation in brick making was the firing of clay.

76
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:01,900
Firing the bricks is more difficult than you might think.

77
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,680
You can't just put them in a bonfire.

78
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,480
That doesn't get them hot enough.

79
00:06:05,481 --> 00:06:11,120
You need to fire them at about 950 to 1,100 degrees centigrade, which is incredibly hot.

80
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:16,120
If you don't get them hot enough, they are soft and they crumble to dust.

81
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:22,220
If you fire them too much, they just turn into a horrible glassy lump and they're completely useless.

82
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:28,140
At the correct temperatures, the clay vitrifies or becomes glass

83
00:06:28,141 --> 00:06:32,520
and fuses with the sand another inert material, making the brick rock solid.

84
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:42,480
The firing brick was mastered around 3000 BC by the ancient Mesopotamians using primitive kilns.

85
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:51,480
The ancient Egyptian builders had a plentiful supply of stone for their grand structures and found no need for this more durable fired brick.

86
00:06:53,480 --> 00:07:02,480
However, both civilizations explored the use of non-clay mortars, primarily lime, gypsum and bitumen.

87
00:07:02,481 --> 00:07:09,020
These mortars sufficiently held bricks together and helped produce some of the most renowned structures in the world.

88
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:14,480
Yet it was the ancient Romans that allowed brick to be exploited to its fullest potential.

89
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:21,320
Now the Romans discovered that if you took volcanic ash and mixed it with lime mortar, you could make a mortar that would dry underwater.

90
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:29,320
Lime mortar is a simple mixture of burnt limestone that has been ground to a white powder, sand and water.

91
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:35,040
By introducing volcanic ash, the mixture gains the ability to dry underwater.

92
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:38,240
This was what we would call a cement.

93
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:45,080
This was hugely advantageous because they could then make harbors that were in the sea, ancient baths, aqueducts, etc.

94
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:50,980
With the fall of the Roman Empire, the technology of Roman cement was lost.

95
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:53,440
But the art of brick making survived.

96
00:07:53,441 --> 00:08:00,060
The Middle Ages saw more experimentation with brick

97
00:08:00,061 --> 00:08:03,320
and the introduction of terracotta

98
00:08:03,321 --> 00:08:05,580
a decorative sculpted brick

99
00:08:05,581 --> 00:08:08,960
There's no difference in medieval terracotta

100
00:08:08,961 --> 00:08:12,340
really between something that is a molded brick

101
00:08:12,341 --> 00:08:13,860
and something that's a piece of terracotta

102
00:08:13,861 --> 00:08:16,460
Through the Middle Ages and into the Renaissance

103
00:08:16,461 --> 00:08:18,080
great achievements in brick

104
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:22,400
including temples, cathedrals, castles, fortifications and city walls

105
00:08:22,401 --> 00:08:23,940
could be found everywhere.

106
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:28,200
But it was the Great Fire of London in 1666

107
00:08:28,201 --> 00:08:30,540
that took brick construction to the next level.

108
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:37,920
17th century London was rambling streets

109
00:08:37,921 --> 00:08:39,200
and tight little alleyways

110
00:08:39,201 --> 00:08:42,000
with timber-framed buildings all packed closely together.

111
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:47,040
On the evening of September 2nd, 1666,

112
00:08:47,640 --> 00:08:50,260
a fire started in the baker's shop on Pudding Lane,

113
00:08:50,261 --> 00:08:53,060
and it quickly spread to neighboring buildings.

114
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:57,980
Samuel Pepys, the diarist, records in his diary

115
00:08:57,981 --> 00:08:59,440
on the evening of the first day,

116
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:01,060
he and some friends went into a boat

117
00:09:01,061 --> 00:09:03,680
and sat on the river watching it.

118
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:07,060
And he said it was almost like watching the first day of hell

119
00:09:07,061 --> 00:09:09,940
that Armageddon had arrived.

120
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:12,380
The fire of London burnt for four days.

121
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:16,780
And in that time, it engulfed the whole of the ancient city

122
00:09:16,781 --> 00:09:18,760
inside the medieval walls.

123
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:23,060
Remarkably, very few people were hurt, and the city of London was devastated.

124
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:25,980
This was the commercial centre of the whole of England.

125
00:09:27,740 --> 00:09:32,440
It was essential for the future of England that London be rebuilt as quickly as possible.

126
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:39,560
And the task of rebuilding the incinerated city fell into the hands of Sir Christopher Wren.

127
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:47,480
Sir Christopher Wren started out as an academic, but quickly came to notice and intellectual circles

128
00:09:47,481 --> 00:09:50,280
and developed as an architect.

129
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:54,340
That's one of the first really recognizable architects in this country.

130
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:59,840
Wren was involved in drafting the new building regulations for London.

131
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:04,960
Regulations decreeing that the city should be rebuilt with a fireproof material.

132
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:07,540
Brick was the obvious choice.

133
00:10:07,541 --> 00:10:14,680
and just outside London

134
00:10:14,681 --> 00:10:16,280
at Bulmer Bricks

135
00:10:16,281 --> 00:10:18,660
a brickwork specializing in made-to-order

136
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:20,300
bricks for restoration projects

137
00:10:20,301 --> 00:10:22,480
many of the brick making techniques

138
00:10:22,481 --> 00:10:23,780
employed to rebuild London

139
00:10:23,781 --> 00:10:25,380
are still in use today

140
00:10:25,381 --> 00:10:29,560
here bricks are still made by hand

141
00:10:29,561 --> 00:10:30,660
using wooden molds

142
00:10:30,661 --> 00:10:35,160
this is the traditional method

143
00:10:35,161 --> 00:10:36,640
basic has been used for centuries

144
00:10:36,641 --> 00:10:45,640
The maker takes a piece of clay, rolls it in sand, and the sand is a means of releasing

145
00:10:45,641 --> 00:10:50,500
it from the mould, and then he can pick his brick up in the mould, takes a board,

146
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:55,520
and just slips it out onto the board.

147
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:02,180
Resands his mould back onto his stock and rolls the next brick.

148
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:05,200
But this is the basic principle that's been used since the time of man.

149
00:11:05,201 --> 00:11:08,480
Once formed, the bricks must be dried.

150
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:14,680
In summer, we wheel the bricks out here to dry naturally in these open air hack rows.

151
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:21,120
And we pitch the bricks on these little concrete paths in order that they will be exposed to the sun and air and dry.

152
00:11:21,420 --> 00:11:25,080
And out here in the summer is about 80,000 bricks drying out here at any one time.

153
00:11:25,081 --> 00:11:35,540
in the winter we're then forced into artificial drying in our drying sheds

154
00:11:35,541 --> 00:11:43,260
once the bricks are fully dried they're brought into the kiln to be fired at temperatures reaching

155
00:11:43,261 --> 00:11:49,780
up to 2 000 degrees it's called a downdraft kiln because the heat is drawn from seven fire boxes

156
00:11:49,781 --> 00:11:54,280
around the outside into the top of the kiln and then down through the bricks that are stacked here

157
00:11:54,281 --> 00:11:58,280
set in a way that allows the heat to percolate down and out of a flue system in the bottom.

158
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:00,840
12,000 come in here at any one time.

159
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:04,720
We complete the loading, seal the door, warm it up for two days,

160
00:12:04,880 --> 00:12:07,400
and then fire for two days, cool it for three days.

161
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:09,040
So it's a continuous process.

162
00:12:09,740 --> 00:12:12,340
Unload as soon as it's cool and repeat the cycle.

163
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,620
We have seven fires around the outside of the kiln.

164
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:19,000
In a regular process, you go around starting at one and going around to seven.

165
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:22,960
The secret is to keep the fires even, depending on when the wind is blowing from the west.

166
00:12:22,961 --> 00:12:26,040
You might find fires are burning faster on one side of the kiln on the other.

167
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:27,820
The whole thing is to keep the thing in balance.

168
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:35,380
By the turn of the 20th century, the demand for brick increased beyond the capacity of this early type of brickwork.

169
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:44,400
Having hit a brick wall, brickmaking was quickly transformed from a handicraft to a mechanized industry.

170
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:50,240
And nowhere would the need for brick be greater than in America's first metropolis.

171
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:52,660
New York City.

172
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:59,140
Some of the bricks in the Great Wall of China are stamped with the name and number of the

173
00:12:59,141 --> 00:13:03,960
army unit responsible for their manufacture, allowing mistakes to be traced back to the

174
00:13:03,961 --> 00:13:06,320
maker.

175
00:13:06,321 --> 00:13:14,980
Bricks will return on Modern Marvels.

176
00:13:14,981 --> 00:13:17,720
We now return to Bricks on Modern Marvels.

177
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:29,820
To make brick, you need lots and lots and lots of clay.

178
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:32,320
But not just any clay will do.

179
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:35,180
You need a special clay, like kaolin clay,

180
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:37,760
which is composed of an aluminum silicate material,

181
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:39,740
essential to making fired brick.

182
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,520
But that's not all.

183
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:47,480
Bricks tend to be made from a mixture of clay and sand

184
00:13:47,481 --> 00:13:48,980
or some other inert material.

185
00:13:48,981 --> 00:13:58,520
If you use pure clay, potter's clay, then the brick is too large an object, and in the kiln it would shrink and crack and break apart.

186
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:07,240
So mud bricks tend to use about 30% clay or even less, and terracotta or pottery at the other end might be 75% clay.

187
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:14,420
Throughout history, brick manufacturers have always located themselves where the clay is.

188
00:14:14,421 --> 00:14:19,240
and brick architecture can always be found somewhere nearby.

189
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:26,980
Since the 19th century and into the early 20th century,

190
00:14:27,980 --> 00:14:33,060
the architecture of New York was predominantly defined by one material, brick.

191
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:38,480
Before the introduction of brick, fire was a great danger to Manhattan.

192
00:14:38,481 --> 00:14:42,420
Great areas of the city were destroyed in the early 19th century

193
00:14:42,421 --> 00:14:49,200
and by the time that the Charlton Street block was constructed, it was illegal to build in wood.

194
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:52,780
Fires were always good for the brick industry.

195
00:14:53,120 --> 00:14:58,740
Its usefulness in terms of fire resistance is one of the most principal reasons for its success as a building material.

196
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:05,400
By the turn of the 20th century, New York was experiencing the greatest growth of any city in the world,

197
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:09,060
a growth that fueled the unprecedented demand for brick.

198
00:15:09,061 --> 00:15:16,980
Basically what happens in New York is as the city's population increases, the population moves north.

199
00:15:16,981 --> 00:15:20,820
And as the population moves north, they build more houses.

200
00:15:21,220 --> 00:15:27,780
Typically in New York during this period, the houses are built on the west side because it was close to the Hudson River waterfront.

201
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:33,460
The Hudson River provided easy, inexpensive transport of bricks to the growing metropolis.

202
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:40,620
But more importantly, it also provided one of the richest clay deposits in the country to the brickmakers upstream.

203
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:44,900
The clay deposits stretch over a distance of over 100 miles.

204
00:15:45,320 --> 00:15:48,460
They're among the largest such deposits in America.

205
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:53,700
There are breaks in it, but the clay deposit region does extend over a considerable distance,

206
00:15:53,701 --> 00:15:55,880
which allowed this tremendous amount of production.

207
00:15:57,160 --> 00:16:02,940
And with a seemingly infinite amount of clay, brick manufacturing became the dominant industry on the Hudson River.

208
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:09,780
By the early 20th century, the Hudson River brick industry boasted over 130 manufacturers.

209
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:14,620
It quickly grew to be the largest brick manufacturing region in the world.

210
00:16:16,440 --> 00:16:25,020
At its zenith, in 1905 and 1906, the Hudson River brick industry manufactured 1.3 billion bricks,

211
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:29,700
which is roughly equivalent to a 40-foot-high wall, 12 inches thick,

212
00:16:29,701 --> 00:16:34,960
with no door or window openings stretching from Albany to Buffalo, which is over 300 miles.

213
00:16:36,320 --> 00:16:38,300
But the zenith came at a price.

214
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:41,440
The Hudson Valley clay deposits were plundered,

215
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:45,840
and overzealous mining to satiate the demand for bricks

216
00:16:45,841 --> 00:16:49,940
led to tragedy in one Hudson River Valley brickyard town.

217
00:16:50,940 --> 00:16:53,580
On the evening of January 8, 1906,

218
00:16:53,581 --> 00:16:57,240
the village of Haverstraw, New York, would pay the ultimate price.

219
00:16:57,241 --> 00:17:06,620
The Excelsior and the Gillies brickyards dug too tight into the town and the roadways.

220
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:08,980
They undermined the streets.

221
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:13,100
An early thaw had defrosted soil and clay beneath the village,

222
00:17:13,620 --> 00:17:16,900
causing portions of the town to be swallowed into the clay pit below.

223
00:17:17,420 --> 00:17:21,200
One third of the village went down with the cave-in.

224
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:26,580
It all happened in a six to seven block area in a crescent shape.

225
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:34,240
The landslide resulted in the deaths of 19 people and the destruction of 20 homes.

226
00:17:34,241 --> 00:17:41,240
Yet despite the tragedy, the brick industry continued to thrive and industrialize.

227
00:17:41,241 --> 00:17:48,240
Moulding of bricks was actually quite slow to mechanize in Europe, but quite fast to

228
00:17:48,241 --> 00:17:52,240
mechanize in the United States where there was a shortage of labor for brick moulding.

229
00:17:52,241 --> 00:17:54,088
The machines worked in two ways.

230
00:17:54,089 --> 00:17:59,460
Either they simulated hand moulding, or the clay was extruded through a die and then cut off.

231
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:08,180
A more fuel-efficient kiln had replaced older intermittent kilns, in which the fire had to be extinguished between firings.

232
00:18:08,920 --> 00:18:11,300
It further increased productivity of the brickyards.

233
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:18,820
The Hoffman kiln worked by having a number of kilns side by side, and moving hot gases between them.

234
00:18:18,821 --> 00:18:24,620
The kiln that was being fired would heat up the next kiln slightly.

235
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:29,540
And by arranging them in a big loop, the kilns could be fired continuously.

236
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:35,580
Many Hoffman kilns have been fired for 50, 75 years continuously without stopping.

237
00:18:36,040 --> 00:18:38,680
And would produce tens of thousands of bricks a day.

238
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:44,700
Hudson River Valley bricks were primarily face brick, or architectural brick,

239
00:18:45,240 --> 00:18:47,740
and encompassed all applications above the ground.

240
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:54,600
But bricks from other parts of the country with denser clay deposits could be used on

241
00:18:54,601 --> 00:18:56,220
the ground as well.

242
00:18:56,221 --> 00:19:01,920
Paving bricks are a very special type of brick that is quite different from building

243
00:19:01,921 --> 00:19:08,940
bricks because they are far denser in order to make them resistant to water absorption

244
00:19:08,941 --> 00:19:14,500
and to give them much greater strength, which they would be required for the beating that

245
00:19:14,501 --> 00:19:16,620
it would take on the ground.

246
00:19:16,621 --> 00:19:19,320
And the road didn't end there.

247
00:19:19,321 --> 00:19:27,380
Industry found uses for a series of specialized bricks, referred to as refractory bricks.

248
00:19:27,381 --> 00:19:32,960
Made from high purity clay, fire clay refractory bricks, which include acid proof brick, can

249
00:19:32,961 --> 00:19:38,220
withstand intense temperatures and were used extensively in furnaces, kilns and other industrial

250
00:19:38,221 --> 00:19:43,940
applications.

251
00:19:43,941 --> 00:19:48,400
The brick construction itself was aided by another innovation, Portland cement.

252
00:19:49,040 --> 00:19:51,980
The big change in mortar came about with the development of Portland cement.

253
00:19:52,740 --> 00:19:56,060
And what it allowed is for mortar to have a greater compressive strength,

254
00:19:56,420 --> 00:20:00,460
so you could fill the voids in the mortar joints between the brick with a thicker joint.

255
00:20:01,660 --> 00:20:08,060
The innovation of Portland cement was patented in 1824 by a bricklayer and mason, Joseph Aspton.

256
00:20:08,061 --> 00:20:15,060
Portland cement contains a pulverized mixture of limestone and clay.

257
00:20:15,061 --> 00:20:21,060
It's heated until it almost fuses and then ground up and mixed with water and sand

258
00:20:21,061 --> 00:20:28,060
to create a very strong mortar, making the brick wall even more durable.

259
00:20:28,061 --> 00:20:33,060
However, a new building block was gaining dominance in construction,

260
00:20:33,061 --> 00:20:37,060
the concrete block, made from Portland cement.

261
00:20:37,061 --> 00:20:44,680
When the cement was mixed with larger aggregates and water, it could be precast to form a block

262
00:20:44,681 --> 00:20:47,500
or poured in place.

263
00:20:47,501 --> 00:20:52,740
When the mixture cured, it became rock hard, creating an inexpensive, accommodating and

264
00:20:52,741 --> 00:20:57,300
durable building material.

265
00:20:57,301 --> 00:21:02,180
By the 1930s, the heyday of the great Hudson River brick industry had come to an abrupt

266
00:21:02,181 --> 00:21:06,300
end.

267
00:21:06,301 --> 00:21:11,360
For Tom Sullivan, who has been hunting old bricks since he was a child, the story of

268
00:21:11,361 --> 00:21:15,400
brick making in the Great Hudson River Valley is preserved by his collection.

269
00:21:15,401 --> 00:21:22,800
Both my families were involved in the brickyards for over 75 years and growing up, that's all

270
00:21:22,801 --> 00:21:25,080
I ever heard was brickyards.

271
00:21:25,081 --> 00:21:28,320
And that moved me on into collecting brick.

272
00:21:28,321 --> 00:21:34,020
And the collection might come from just walking along the river where the yards used to be,

273
00:21:34,021 --> 00:21:39,240
or maybe a building that was being torn down, or a sidewalk, and then a few other members

274
00:21:39,241 --> 00:21:43,320
of the family knew I was collecting and they would drop a few off occasionally.

275
00:21:43,321 --> 00:21:49,700
The thing is, a lot of the brands were unique, and what I collect is bricks that have a brand,

276
00:21:49,701 --> 00:21:53,860
a name, or a initial, or a symbol on them.

277
00:21:53,861 --> 00:21:55,420
That's what makes them unique.

278
00:21:55,421 --> 00:22:02,020
If you get a brick, like here, W and A, that's Wood and Allison.

279
00:22:02,021 --> 00:22:06,260
The brick with raised letters is much older.

280
00:22:06,261 --> 00:22:11,200
They really didn't start marking the brands until around 1870.

281
00:22:11,201 --> 00:22:14,360
Something like this with the raised letters, that's a rare bird.

282
00:22:14,361 --> 00:22:17,480
That's rarity.

283
00:22:17,481 --> 00:22:23,640
The most common brick that has what we call a frog, and the idea of the frog came from

284
00:22:23,641 --> 00:22:24,640
England.

285
00:22:24,641 --> 00:22:29,260
It eliminated some of the material, made the brick lighter, and when you laid the brick

286
00:22:29,261 --> 00:22:35,257
down into cement, it helped to bond the brick better. And they call it a frog.

287
00:22:35,258 --> 00:22:36,180
Some of them

288
00:22:36,181 --> 00:22:37,763
were spelled backwards.

289
00:22:37,764 --> 00:22:42,720
Whoever made the wooden mold, they put the letters in so they could read

290
00:22:42,721 --> 00:22:47,954
them, not realizing when they put clay in there, it's going to be reversed.

291
00:22:47,955 --> 00:22:49,140
So there's such a

292
00:22:49,141 --> 00:22:55,360
variety of brands, even a brick like Shanky here, part of it's missing, but until I get a whole

293
00:22:55,361 --> 00:22:57,320
Shanky brick, I'm going to keep that.

294
00:22:59,260 --> 00:23:04,320
The story of the brickyards can also be found in the brick and mortar structures that once dominated the city.

295
00:23:05,240 --> 00:23:09,180
The legacy of the Hudson River brick industry is right down there in New York.

296
00:23:09,181 --> 00:23:12,860
All you have to do is to walk around and keep your eyes open.

297
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:22,480
All of the skyscrapers, you know, their steel frame construction, the facade itself, was infilled with brick or terracotta.

298
00:23:22,860 --> 00:23:26,640
And that didn't change until well into the 1930s.

299
00:23:26,641 --> 00:23:30,380
and then we move towards the steel and glass.

300
00:23:30,381 --> 00:23:33,840
The glass curtain wall really doesn't formally come on the scene

301
00:23:33,841 --> 00:23:36,480
until the post-war years after World War II.

302
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:41,440
Today, the look of New York City and other American cities

303
00:23:41,441 --> 00:23:43,800
has become glass, steel, and concrete.

304
00:23:47,180 --> 00:23:51,160
But the preferred look of middle America is still brick and mortar.

305
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:56,160
Even though, you know, we're comfortable with steel and glass and concrete

306
00:23:56,161 --> 00:23:59,320
in an office setting or an industrialized setting.

307
00:23:59,840 --> 00:24:04,120
Most Americans don't feel particularly comfortable with that in their homes.

308
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:07,520
Many people feel that the modern aesthetic can be a little cool.

309
00:24:07,940 --> 00:24:10,380
So when we come home at the end of the night,

310
00:24:10,500 --> 00:24:14,480
we're typically, you know, wanting some of that warmth,

311
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:16,460
and brick tends to give us that.

312
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:21,100
And that desire fuels a demand for the billions of bricks

313
00:24:21,101 --> 00:24:23,100
that are sold in the United States every year.

314
00:24:23,101 --> 00:24:33,400
And to assure that they wouldn't be a few bricks short of a load, high-tech reinforcements would have to be enlisted to get the job done.

315
00:24:36,920 --> 00:24:40,743
Approximately 96% of bricks manufactured in the U.S.

316
00:24:40,744 --> 00:24:47,140
are face brick, 2% are paving brick, and the remaining 2% are all other brick products.

317
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:50,500
Bricks will return on Modern Marvels.

318
00:24:50,501 --> 00:24:55,620
We now return to Bricks on Modern Marvels.

319
00:24:58,320 --> 00:25:03,520
In the United States, the brick industry is able to produce over 8 billion bricks in a year.

320
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:10,760
And in order to create this abundance of brick, today's factories have become even more sophisticated.

321
00:25:10,761 --> 00:25:21,640
To make brick in a big amount, you need a big amount of clay.

322
00:25:21,641 --> 00:25:27,140
We use over 100,000 tons a year.

323
00:25:27,141 --> 00:25:31,480
The raw earth and clay are put through a grinder to be pulverized before they can be processed

324
00:25:31,481 --> 00:25:34,240
into brick.

325
00:25:34,241 --> 00:25:38,200
It's at this stage that additives can be introduced to the raw material to alter the

326
00:25:38,201 --> 00:25:42,520
color of the brick later in the process. There are several features in the

327
00:25:42,521 --> 00:25:46,580
manufacturing process that affect the color of brick. Primarily of course is

328
00:25:46,581 --> 00:25:51,080
just the chemicals in the raw materials that are involved. Iron is probably the

329
00:25:51,081 --> 00:25:55,420
most common metal or material that's involved in the manufacturing process.

330
00:25:55,421 --> 00:26:00,280
It gives brick its characteristic red color that so many people think of. But

331
00:26:00,281 --> 00:26:04,300
that color can be modified by adding other chemicals such as manganese to

332
00:26:04,301 --> 00:26:10,600
produce a brown colored brick or other raw materials without iron in that clay or shale

333
00:26:10,601 --> 00:26:13,247
will fire to a buff or a lighter color.

334
00:26:13,248 --> 00:26:16,980
But at this rudimentary stage, the color of the mixture

335
00:26:16,981 --> 00:26:20,676
offers little clue as to the color of the final brick.

336
00:26:20,677 --> 00:26:23,620
The brown clay mixture enters an industrial

337
00:26:23,621 --> 00:26:28,553
blender, referred to as a pug mill, where water is introduced.

338
00:26:28,554 --> 00:26:30,940
It's conveyed down here to the

339
00:26:30,941 --> 00:26:36,363
vacuum chamber and down into the extruder below.

340
00:26:36,364 --> 00:26:41,560
Inside the extruder is a long screw-like auger

341
00:26:41,561 --> 00:26:45,541
that pushes the clay forward under a great force.

342
00:26:45,542 --> 00:26:49,440
The dense clay mixture is extruded through a dye

343
00:26:49,441 --> 00:26:52,412
where the size of the brick is determined.

344
00:26:52,413 --> 00:26:56,020
In addition, core holes or circular cavities inside

345
00:26:56,021 --> 00:26:58,452
the column are produced by the die.

346
00:26:58,453 --> 00:27:02,900
The advantage to coring bricks is that they fire more uniformly.

347
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:08,980
They also become easier to handle, less expensive to ship, and create a better bond with the mortar

348
00:27:08,981 --> 00:27:14,721
when laid. The bricks coming out of the extruder at a rate at about a ton a minute.

349
00:27:14,722 --> 00:27:15,620
The brick are

350
00:27:15,621 --> 00:27:17,163
stiff but still soft.

351
00:27:17,164 --> 00:27:22,820
They go to a cutter here, the slug cutter, where they're cut into long slugs.

352
00:27:22,821 --> 00:27:30,700
The 65-inch long slugs are moved into position on a conveyor belt and stopped using laser

353
00:27:30,701 --> 00:27:31,700
sensors.

354
00:27:31,701 --> 00:27:37,160
They're then moved forward into position to be pushed hydraulically through wires to form

355
00:27:37,161 --> 00:27:40,000
the final brick shape.

356
00:27:40,001 --> 00:27:44,840
And in much less time than it would take to produce one handmade brick, over 50 bricks

357
00:27:44,841 --> 00:27:47,000
are formed and ready to be stacked.

358
00:27:47,001 --> 00:27:50,900
This is where we stack the brick on the cars.

359
00:27:50,901 --> 00:27:53,100
These bricks are wet.

360
00:27:53,101 --> 00:27:56,800
They're stacked by these state-of-the-art robots.

361
00:27:56,801 --> 00:28:02,400
Years ago, the bricks were stacked by hand, then evolved to a crude kind of machinery.

362
00:28:02,401 --> 00:28:07,700
These are the most modern kind of machines you can get now.

363
00:28:07,701 --> 00:28:11,800
The robots are autonomous and operate without a human controller.

364
00:28:11,801 --> 00:28:16,700
We can stack about 20,000 bricks in an hour.

365
00:28:16,701 --> 00:28:19,200
Silne cars advance through the factory on tracks.

366
00:28:20,300 --> 00:28:23,520
And their next destination, the holding room,

367
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,760
where temperatures are kept at nearly 90 degrees,

368
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:30,980
with a 90% relative humidity to keep the bricks from drying prematurely.

369
00:28:33,180 --> 00:28:37,680
We can produce the brick much faster than we can dry or fire it.

370
00:28:38,040 --> 00:28:40,640
So we store it in these rooms basically wet

371
00:28:40,641 --> 00:28:43,920
to condition it before we put it in the drying ovens.

372
00:28:43,921 --> 00:28:47,920
The bricks at this point are still soft enough to break.

373
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:52,920
The cars are parked here until room becomes available in the dryer.

374
00:28:56,920 --> 00:28:59,920
This is the kiln and dryer control room.

375
00:28:59,921 --> 00:29:03,920
Here we monitor the temperatures and pressures in the kiln.

376
00:29:03,921 --> 00:29:07,920
The tunnel kiln is basically a 300-foot-long oven.

377
00:29:07,921 --> 00:29:12,920
You can see on these gauges some of the temperatures as you ramp up through the tunnel.

378
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:16,980
The middle part of the tunnel is the hot part.

379
00:29:17,880 --> 00:29:21,680
Right now our maximum temperatures are around 1860 Fahrenheit.

380
00:29:24,420 --> 00:29:28,640
The tunnel kiln is in operation 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

381
00:29:30,500 --> 00:29:35,040
Cars enter the kiln and are pushed forward every half hour by the length of half a car.

382
00:29:35,980 --> 00:29:39,020
The bricks move through the middle of the kiln for several hours

383
00:29:39,021 --> 00:29:41,400
so that the heat can penetrate the bricks thoroughly.

384
00:29:41,401 --> 00:29:48,400
It's here that the alumina silica clay vitrifies, or becomes glass, and fuses with the sand and other inert material.

385
00:29:48,401 --> 00:29:55,400
If you fire a brick too hot, it will actually turn to a type of glass and melt.

386
00:29:55,401 --> 00:30:04,400
We fire it just to the point of vitrification to where we get the optimum strength and low water absorption.

387
00:30:04,401 --> 00:30:10,400
With the particular clay at this factory, that temperature is around 1900 degrees Fahrenheit.

388
00:30:11,400 --> 00:30:19,700
To get the kiln to these temperature extremes, 120 programmable natural gas burners are located on the top and sides of the kiln.

389
00:30:21,640 --> 00:30:27,500
They create the inferno inside and determine the environment that each kiln car passes through.

390
00:30:28,840 --> 00:30:34,040
If bricks that contain sufficient iron are fired at lower temperatures, they will become a pink color.

391
00:30:35,160 --> 00:30:37,720
As the temperatures rise, they become a deep red.

392
00:30:37,721 --> 00:30:42,720
At higher temperatures, they become a burgundy or a deep purple color.

393
00:30:42,721 --> 00:30:51,720
And in a reducing atmosphere where there is less oxygen used in the firing process, the bricks become a deep black and can have a bluish tinge to them.

394
00:30:54,720 --> 00:31:00,720
In just 24 hours from their entry into the tunnel kiln, the bricks are ready to be shipped out.

395
00:31:00,721 --> 00:31:06,720
This is almost the last part of the operation here where they're unloading the bricks from the cars.

396
00:31:06,721 --> 00:31:12,420
They put the bricks on the pallets and sort out the few bad ones.

397
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:14,780
Then they'll be wrapped and put into inventory.

398
00:31:16,220 --> 00:31:18,380
This is how it's done at Castaic Creek.

399
00:31:19,320 --> 00:31:23,840
But in other factories, robots have already completely taken over these tasks.

400
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:26,600
We see robots coming into the brick plants.

401
00:31:27,360 --> 00:31:32,160
And now it's common for the brick to go from their initial manufacturing process,

402
00:31:32,161 --> 00:31:38,760
starting out as the raw material into packages shipped to the job site without ever being touched by humans.

403
00:31:39,220 --> 00:31:43,540
The bricklayer is the first one who actually puts their hands on the brick when they place them in the wall.

404
00:31:44,860 --> 00:31:48,100
But robots won't be replacing bricklayers anytime soon,

405
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:53,740
as you have to be much more than just another brick in the wall to become a bricklayer.

406
00:31:53,741 --> 00:32:03,300
With its 318-foot tunnel kiln, Castaic Brick is able to produce close to 500 tons of brick

407
00:32:03,301 --> 00:32:04,300
a day.

408
00:32:04,301 --> 00:32:07,180
Bricks will return on Modern Marvels.

409
00:32:07,181 --> 00:32:18,340
We now return to Bricks on Modern Marvels.

410
00:32:18,341 --> 00:32:22,100
In order to create a brick wall, you need a brick layer.

411
00:32:22,101 --> 00:32:26,600
One of the unique things about construction in general is that each project is different.

412
00:32:26,601 --> 00:32:29,540
If you're making widgets in a factory, you're concerned about that process.

413
00:32:29,541 --> 00:32:32,740
You know, tomorrow I'm making this widget a little better than I made this widget today.

414
00:32:32,741 --> 00:32:36,600
But if you're going to build a construction project, you're setting up a factory right

415
00:32:36,601 --> 00:32:39,980
there and the next one's going to be totally different, the next one totally different.

416
00:32:39,981 --> 00:32:42,320
So you need that human touch.

417
00:32:42,321 --> 00:32:48,160
We constantly investigate mechanical ways to make a craft worker's life easier, but

418
00:32:48,161 --> 00:32:52,280
But every time you look at some of these, you come back to the most efficient way to

419
00:32:52,281 --> 00:32:55,700
lay a brick is by hand with a trowel.

420
00:32:55,701 --> 00:33:01,280
Today, the Bricklayers Union offers a challenging training course for the newest generation

421
00:33:01,281 --> 00:33:07,920
entering the trowel trades.

422
00:33:07,921 --> 00:33:11,440
Here students work, eat and sleep on campus.

423
00:33:11,441 --> 00:33:14,280
And for 12 weeks, brick becomes their life.

424
00:33:14,281 --> 00:33:19,280
You have nice places to stay, you got barracks, you got chow hall, they provide your food.

425
00:33:19,281 --> 00:33:23,280
And I enjoy it, I don't mind, I like it.

426
00:33:23,281 --> 00:33:27,280
The typical training day is from 7.30 a.m. to 4 p.m.

427
00:33:27,281 --> 00:33:30,280
And the curriculum is intense.

428
00:33:30,281 --> 00:33:33,280
And once you get here, they teach you all the basics to brickwork.

429
00:33:33,281 --> 00:33:38,280
They show you about the mortar and the different types of brick and different kinds of bonds you use.

430
00:33:38,281 --> 00:33:45,240
A bond is the arrangement of bricks in a pattern intended to increase the strength or just enhance the appearance of a wall.

431
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:52,820
This is an English bond, and what we have is alternating courses of stretchers and then headers.

432
00:33:53,580 --> 00:33:55,450
It's a very strong bond.

433
00:33:55,451 --> 00:34:00,360
It's strong mostly because this header ties this wall together.

434
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:02,330
This is a similar bond.

435
00:34:02,331 --> 00:34:06,720
It's a Flemish bond, also a very strong bond, and very decorative.

436
00:34:06,721 --> 00:34:13,440
And what we have here is a header, stretcher, header, stretcher, and then they alternate

437
00:34:13,441 --> 00:34:19,040
on the next course so your header is in the center of your stretcher.

438
00:34:19,041 --> 00:34:21,160
But it doesn't stop there.

439
00:34:21,161 --> 00:34:27,360
Students learn everything from constructing an arch, cutting masonry units, building piers,

440
00:34:27,361 --> 00:34:30,420
and laying refractory products.

441
00:34:30,421 --> 00:34:37,980
That is in addition to setting a line, furrowing the mortar, laying a course, and tooling a

442
00:34:37,981 --> 00:34:38,980
joint.

443
00:34:38,981 --> 00:34:42,140
It is pretty serious.

444
00:34:42,141 --> 00:34:45,580
We're here all day long, except for a 30-minute lunch, that's it.

445
00:34:45,581 --> 00:34:47,920
Other than that, we're in here and we're always on something.

446
00:34:47,921 --> 00:34:51,300
As soon as we finish the project, we take it down and we start another project.

447
00:34:51,301 --> 00:34:55,820
And we just keep moving on and keep moving on and just keep progressing and progressing.

448
00:34:55,821 --> 00:34:58,820
Because of course, it's tougher than it looks to lay a course.

449
00:34:58,821 --> 00:35:03,560
If somebody was telling me, oh, it's easy, I could do that, I'd give them the trowel, give them the mud, say, here you go.

450
00:35:04,160 --> 00:35:04,960
Go ahead and do it.

451
00:35:05,660 --> 00:35:06,640
And I'd laugh at them.

452
00:35:06,940 --> 00:35:07,880
Because it's not that easy.

453
00:35:07,980 --> 00:35:10,860
I mean, it looks easy, and I thought it was easy when I first saw it done.

454
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:12,060
It's a lot of hard work.

455
00:35:13,820 --> 00:35:18,500
The program is just a starting point for aspiring bricklayers before they enter the real world.

456
00:35:22,180 --> 00:35:25,660
Where bricklayers have been building brick walls for countless generations.

457
00:35:25,661 --> 00:35:32,260
At first glance, their walls may look similar, but they too have evolved.

458
00:35:32,261 --> 00:35:36,300
It's kind of funny that things didn't change for a long time in brick.

459
00:35:36,301 --> 00:35:40,840
You know, you looked at older structures, there's big thick stone or brick walls,

460
00:35:40,841 --> 00:35:47,060
and then as you move through the 19th century, we got to a pinnacle would be the Monadnock building in Chicago.

461
00:35:47,720 --> 00:35:52,200
The Monadnock marks the last of the brick load-bearing wall structures,

462
00:35:52,201 --> 00:35:55,040
and it exhibits the problem with all such structures.

463
00:35:55,220 --> 00:35:56,580
The walls need to be very thick.

464
00:35:57,000 --> 00:35:59,700
In fact, at the base of the Monadnock building, they're over six foot thick

465
00:35:59,701 --> 00:36:02,040
and that the building itself becomes very heavy,

466
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:04,120
so heavy in the case of the Monadnock building

467
00:36:04,121 --> 00:36:07,580
that it actually sank into the pavement and you have to step down into it.

468
00:36:08,040 --> 00:36:11,000
Thereafter, it's realized that tall buildings can no longer be constructed

469
00:36:11,001 --> 00:36:13,640
with brick load-bearing walls.

470
00:36:15,180 --> 00:36:19,140
A load-bearing wall consists of bricks laid several thicknesses deep.

471
00:36:19,141 --> 00:36:24,280
The bricks below support the entire structure of brick and mortar above it.

472
00:36:25,420 --> 00:36:28,480
With the advent of reinforced concrete and steel framing,

473
00:36:29,520 --> 00:36:32,760
the reinforced concrete or steel frames support the weight of the building,

474
00:36:33,220 --> 00:36:35,360
and bricks become relegated to a veneer.

475
00:36:36,540 --> 00:36:41,060
You don't have these huge mass walls that we had, you know, 100 years ago or before that.

476
00:36:41,061 --> 00:36:48,900
You have more of a thin wall system, which might just be one, you know, one thickness of brick or one wife of brick.

477
00:36:49,140 --> 00:36:52,260
with that airspace and with the concrete block for the structure.

478
00:36:53,200 --> 00:36:57,440
And even though today's brick walls may no longer be the workhorses they once were,

479
00:36:58,060 --> 00:36:59,440
they're not wallpaper either.

480
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:02,580
They provide a durable barrier from the wrath of Mother Nature.

481
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:10,700
This past year, we decided to do some testing to show how good brick was in resisting wind-borne missiles.

482
00:37:13,900 --> 00:37:17,660
Hurricanes, of course, can generate a lot of debris that flies through the air,

483
00:37:17,661 --> 00:37:20,100
and that debris can damage siding materials.

484
00:37:20,540 --> 00:37:22,580
There are standards that have been developed

485
00:37:22,581 --> 00:37:26,280
to evaluate the performance of siding materials

486
00:37:26,281 --> 00:37:28,380
in protecting the occupants of buildings.

487
00:37:28,920 --> 00:37:32,300
We used a fiber cement board, we used vinyl siding,

488
00:37:32,540 --> 00:37:35,700
and we used standard face brick as the exterior.

489
00:37:35,701 --> 00:37:39,560
That nine-pound missile at 34 miles per hour

490
00:37:39,561 --> 00:37:42,920
easily pierced the fiber cement board siding

491
00:37:42,921 --> 00:37:46,020
through the insulation and through the drywall

492
00:37:46,021 --> 00:37:49,280
and projected about five feet into the occupied space.

493
00:37:49,940 --> 00:37:52,540
That same thing occurred with the vinyl siding.

494
00:37:56,920 --> 00:38:01,000
That eight-foot two-by-four simply bounced off the surface of the brick wall,

495
00:38:01,240 --> 00:38:02,680
and that passed the test.

496
00:38:04,660 --> 00:38:07,300
Hurricanes may be no match for the mighty brick wall,

497
00:38:07,720 --> 00:38:11,500
but other forces of nature can bring it tumbling down.

498
00:38:12,400 --> 00:38:14,460
And with smart bricks entering the picture,

499
00:38:14,461 --> 00:38:18,360
being thick as a brick may no longer apply.

500
00:38:21,080 --> 00:38:24,040
A standard concrete block is intentionally equivalent

501
00:38:24,041 --> 00:38:27,240
to the dimensions of three standard bricks with mortar joints.

502
00:38:27,660 --> 00:38:29,800
This allows them to match up modularly

503
00:38:29,801 --> 00:38:32,800
so you don't have to cut the bricks when you come to the end of a wall.

504
00:38:33,680 --> 00:38:35,520
Bricks will return on Modern Marvels.

505
00:38:36,920 --> 00:38:40,020
We now return to Bricks on Modern Marvels.

506
00:38:44,460 --> 00:38:47,260
Tuesday, October 17, 1989.

507
00:38:48,480 --> 00:38:54,740
Without warning, a massive earthquake measuring 6.9 on the Richter scale strikes San Francisco, California.

508
00:38:55,340 --> 00:38:59,460
Prepare yourselves. Shut off the gas. Shut off electricity.

509
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:01,720
Prepare for aftershocks.

510
00:39:02,180 --> 00:39:05,140
Make sure you're building a sound enough to go back inside.

511
00:39:06,220 --> 00:39:10,600
It lasts only 15 seconds, but leaves parts of the city in ruins.

512
00:39:10,601 --> 00:39:17,600
Tons of bricks rained down upon the streets during the evening rush hour, some hitting pedestrians.

513
00:39:17,601 --> 00:39:25,600
In the aftermath of the quake, San Francisco was determined to retrofit its over 2,000 unreinforced masonry structures.

514
00:39:30,600 --> 00:39:38,600
The result of the Loma Prieta earthquake in 1989 was a citywide ordinance requiring that all unreinforced masonry buildings be seismically strengthened.

515
00:39:38,601 --> 00:39:45,000
The Wool & Mill building being built in 1864 obviously put it into that category of being

516
00:39:45,001 --> 00:39:48,640
unreinforced masonry and was going to be one of the buildings that we would need to strengthen

517
00:39:48,641 --> 00:39:50,700
here at Ghirardelli Square.

518
00:39:50,701 --> 00:39:54,680
The buildings which we are particularly concerned with are known as UMBs, which is unreinforced

519
00:39:54,681 --> 00:39:57,020
masonry bearing wall buildings.

520
00:39:57,021 --> 00:40:00,280
And the reason they are the worst case is because by definition a bearing wall is the

521
00:40:00,281 --> 00:40:02,160
one that holds the structure up.

522
00:40:02,161 --> 00:40:08,360
If the bearing wall fails, then the whole structure can come down and that will be catastrophic.

523
00:40:08,361 --> 00:40:12,180
engineers use several methods to seismically upgrade unreinforced buildings.

524
00:40:13,700 --> 00:40:16,620
Conventionally, a lot of buildings have been done with steel bracing.

525
00:40:16,840 --> 00:40:19,080
People started doing that, I guess, 20, 30 years ago.

526
00:40:19,720 --> 00:40:21,480
There have been a lot of buildings done with shotcrete,

527
00:40:21,620 --> 00:40:23,320
which is where they literally spray concrete,

528
00:40:23,480 --> 00:40:25,180
and that provides additional strength.

529
00:40:26,220 --> 00:40:28,880
More recently, people have been looking for less intrusive ways

530
00:40:28,881 --> 00:40:30,600
of strengthening these buildings.

531
00:40:31,880 --> 00:40:35,200
And the latest technological advancement in seismic retrofitting

532
00:40:35,201 --> 00:40:37,560
is an epoxy fiber wrap composite.

533
00:40:38,360 --> 00:40:44,520
The procedure strengthens brick walls by coating them with thin layers of fiberglass fabric and epoxy.

534
00:40:46,340 --> 00:40:50,480
The composite penetrates the brick and mortar and bonds them tightly together.

535
00:40:51,260 --> 00:40:56,560
It also provides tensile strength, which allows the normally rigid wall to bend without breaking apart.

536
00:40:58,720 --> 00:41:02,260
Ghirardelli's woolen mill building is the proving ground for this new technology.

537
00:41:03,060 --> 00:41:06,060
The fiberglass is applied with rolls of fiberglass fabric

538
00:41:06,061 --> 00:41:08,800
and then is hardened via an epoxy application.

539
00:41:09,740 --> 00:41:13,220
The fiberglass still is part of an overall composite system

540
00:41:13,221 --> 00:41:17,260
which includes steel horizontal members that are bolted into the strengthened wall.

541
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:21,160
On the lower floors, the fiberglass encompasses the entire wall of brick,

542
00:41:21,161 --> 00:41:24,800
but as we move up through the building, the need for the fiberglass decreases.

543
00:41:27,060 --> 00:41:30,780
As you see, we have a narrow strip of the fiberglass adjacent to the windows.

544
00:41:30,781 --> 00:41:35,640
The windows are the weakest point of the building where we have perimeter openings in the brick wall itself.

545
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:40,400
Being one of the first to utilize this revolutionary system in San Francisco,

546
00:41:41,020 --> 00:41:44,320
the woolen mill retrofit is opening doors for this new technology.

547
00:41:46,180 --> 00:41:51,880
Girardelli Square has a number of buildings on the property, some of which still need to be seismically strengthened.

548
00:41:52,540 --> 00:41:55,760
This was the first unreinforced masonry building to be strengthened,

549
00:41:55,761 --> 00:42:02,360
and we will look at the technologies used here as we make decisions going forward to seismically strengthen the other buildings.

550
00:42:03,560 --> 00:42:07,240
I think it would find more widespread use than it has at the moment.

551
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:14,480
I think as systems go, it works very well, and it certainly helps the buildings in their behavior.

552
00:42:15,120 --> 00:42:20,200
Unlike the steel frames or the concrete, which tends to fight against the brickwork, it actually works with it.

553
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:22,040
That makes it very appealing.

554
00:42:22,041 --> 00:42:29,040
Today's epoxy fiber wrap composites may be the salvation for unreinforced brick buildings

555
00:42:29,041 --> 00:42:31,040
when the next big one hits.

556
00:42:31,041 --> 00:42:38,040
And in the future, smart brick technology developed at the Micro Actuators Sensors and Systems Group

557
00:42:38,041 --> 00:42:45,040
at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign may assist engineers to locate structural weaknesses afterwards.

558
00:42:45,041 --> 00:42:53,260
One recent technique that was developed is the embedment of a computer chip into brick,

559
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:55,360
which is then placed into the building.

560
00:42:55,980 --> 00:43:02,480
These smart brick can provide information on the temperature variations that the brick is going through,

561
00:43:03,140 --> 00:43:09,380
what type of vibration might be coming as a result of wind load or other loadings on the building itself.

562
00:43:09,381 --> 00:43:14,940
They can provide information that might indicate deterioration of the wall assembly,

563
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:18,185
buildup of moisture, things of that nature.

564
00:43:18,186 --> 00:43:22,280
So the brick serves as a means to provide those sensors,

565
00:43:22,281 --> 00:43:27,620
those chips, into the building so that the engineers can analyze the data that they're

566
00:43:27,621 --> 00:43:30,060
able to collect from that source.

567
00:43:30,061 --> 00:43:33,980
Several embedded bricks at different locations around

568
00:43:33,981 --> 00:43:38,660
the building would form a network and submit information wirelessly to a remote computer.

569
00:43:38,661 --> 00:43:44,860
These high-tech bricks cap a legacy thousands of years in the making.

570
00:43:45,640 --> 00:43:48,620
It's just burnt clay, but it has shaped our world.

571
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:54,320
And it's no wonder that we have developed a love affair with this seemingly humble material.

572
00:43:55,360 --> 00:43:59,600
From when we're young children, we play with bricks and blocks,

573
00:43:59,960 --> 00:44:04,720
and we learn about the three little pigs and how that last house made of brick

574
00:44:04,721 --> 00:44:06,820
couldn't huff or puff or blow that house down.

575
00:44:06,821 --> 00:44:10,320
So, brick is always part of our culture.

576
00:44:10,321 --> 00:44:13,520
You follow the yellow brick road, you know, you follow your dreams.

577
00:44:13,521 --> 00:44:18,020
It may have a very long history, but it's a history of mass production.

578
00:44:18,021 --> 00:44:21,620
So the brick is, if you like, stands for the modern age.

579
00:44:21,621 --> 00:44:25,820
What it shows us is that modern isn't nearly as modern as we think.

580
00:44:25,821 --> 00:44:30,120
I see no reason why a man should stop using a brick.

581
00:44:30,121 --> 00:44:33,820
In fact, it might come back in, as we have to husband our resources more,

582
00:44:33,821 --> 00:44:36,620
it might in fact become more important than it is at the present time.

